A||||16||IwannaIRM||Best Springs for the money?||||5||
Z||000000||IwannaIRM||08-07-2001||10:29 AM||||Ok, all you techies.  Who do you think offers the best performance springs on the market for the money?<P>I am looking for the most bang for the buck here.<P>I am looking at the Eibach's but only because I am familiar with the brand.  Please open my mind to other brands and give me your honest opinions of their springs.<P>Thanks,<P>Steve||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||08-07-2001||10:37 AM||||Eibachs and Suspension Tech's are the popular choice's. From what I have personally rode in, (I have the Eibach's and a friend has S.T.'s) The differences are price. Just search around for who sells them for the best price. The quality of both are very good.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||pherder||08-07-2001||11:01 AM||||JC Whitless has the ST springs for sale (for 84-87 Fieros) for ~$70 a pair.<P>Best price I have seen lately.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000003||IwannaIRM||08-07-2001||11:22 AM||||Well looks like Suspension Technologies are $79/pair.  But they give two different drop dimensions.  1/2"(A) and 2"(B).  Does this mean they have different springs or am I to interpret this as the front drops 1/2" and the back drops 2" or vice-versa?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||08-07-2001||11:41 AM||||They are 2 different sets of springs. The 2" drop ones are stiffer (higher spring rate)<BR>Eibachs also have different rates too.<BR>For all-around daily driving the 1/2" drops are good. The 2" drops are very stiff so you will feel the bumps more. I have the Eibachs with the 1" drop. I also changed the bushings to poly and use KYB shocks/struts. The suspension is VERY stiff. There is very little roll if any.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DreXteR||08-07-2001||01:41 PM||||I have Intrax springs, and lower the car 1.5" , but it appears to be 2 much. BTW, only I done the front, the rear is out of the car. I wish have a digi camera to take pics.<P>L8tr<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000006||TI_3VOM||08-07-2001||03:13 PM||||...while we're on this subject, what's available for the '88s?||||reg||5
Z||000007||IwannaIRM||08-07-2001||03:46 PM||||Well, I think I will stay with the Eibachs.  Already went for the KYB shocks and struts and I just bought a complete poly bushing kit.<P>Maybe my Fiero will out corner my Firehawk?  I hope not, then I have to justify why I bought the hawk. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||08-07-2001||04:08 PM||||Since when are firehawks , or any F body for that matter known for cornering???<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Black88GT||08-07-2001||04:20 PM||||For 88s, the options are Eibach or Fiero Store springs.  I have heard the Eibachs dont lower as much as the Fiero Stores do.  Eibach is only sold at <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com" TARGET=_blank>www.americancustomeng.com</A>  (for 88s)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||IwannaIRM||08-07-2001||04:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Since when are firehawks , or any F body for that matter known for cornering???</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Since it comes with the Bilstein suspension and 17" wheel upgrade.  Drops the car about an inch or more.  And this bad boy car will hug corners like you would'nt believe.<P>Plus it comes with stock rating of 335hp.<P>I know I could put this in my Fiero but, I like my Hawk to be the beast in the garage and on the streets.||||reg||1
